Databases

In this section I learned that one of the major categories used to classify databases is 
whether they're SQL or NoSQL. A database that uses the SQL language means data must have some type of relation 
to one another in order for it to be processed. This is good for multi-row transactions. NoSQL on the other hand
allows for unstructured data. This is good when working with JSON-heavy workflows. 

COBOL 

I learned that COBOL is a language that is verbose but holds an easy-to-follow hierarchy. Every column represents certain characters 
in a string of words, which makes it easy to follow. Most ATMs and credit card transactions run on COBOL today (DEV Community June 2026), making 
it a language with longevity. Some in the developer community have wanted to replace it because its hierachy and 600 commands are cumbersome for some people. 
However developers are reluctant to do this due to how complicated and risky it would be to do so. This makes the field relatively stable in the long term. 


SWIFT 
I learned that SWIFT is a foreign exchange messaging service used to channel money between countries. Banks hold vostro and nostro accounts with each other's
currency so that they can readily dispense money once the SWIFT message comes. SWIFT has faced competition in recent years from Chinese alternatives. Stablecoins 
have also appeared through blockchains as a way to settle money. Blockchains are competitive as conventional SWIFT transctions take multiple days to settle between 
countries. 


APIs
An API is an Application Programming Interface. There are different APIs such as REST, GraphQK, gRPC, and WebSocket. However, REST APIs are the dominant style. This 
is because REST APIs are versatile when attempting to connect applications and web pages with cloud-based servers. Some of the main benefits of a REST API are the simple, standardized
approach to communication and its scalability/statelessness. In addition to not having to worry about formatting every single time I do a workflow, I am able to make modifications more 
easily when my service grows. 


Intersection of COBOL, SWIFT, APIs & databases

In this section I learned that COBOL is a language that is seen as very useful but cumbersome by developers. 
APIs are a way to simplify transactions that go through COBOL databases. They are also being used to route formats of COBOL origin through SWIFT networks. 
A cool thing about APIs is that they do not need to rewrite or overhaul a process. Many APIs are able to copy the most cumbersome parts of 
workflows and condense them to an in-line process that saves time and still produces high quality results. 